1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,020
Why does cutting an onion make you cry?

2
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:06,900
A whole onion can sit on your counter for weeks and never bother you.

3
00:00:06,790 --> 00:00:09,660
That's because its cells keep two things apart:

4
00:00:09,590 --> 00:00:13,073
sulfur compounds on one side, and an enzyme on the other.

5
00:00:12,977 --> 00:00:15,940
But the moment your knife goes in, those cells burst,

6
00:00:15,830 --> 00:00:17,700
and the two spill together.

7
00:00:17,590 --> 00:00:21,580
The enzyme snips the sulfur compounds into an unstable acid.

8
00:00:21,550 --> 00:00:25,820
For decades, scientists thought that acid became tear gas by itself.

9
00:00:25,990 --> 00:00:29,820
Then, in two thousand two, a team in Japan found a second enzyme

10
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:32,460
whose whole job is making that gas.

11
00:00:32,390 --> 00:00:36,620
It's called syn-propanethial-S-oxide. Quite a mouthful.

12
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:39,633
It evaporates fast and drifts toward your face.

13
00:00:39,537 --> 00:00:42,620
On your eye, it sets off the nerves that sense pain.

14
00:00:42,550 --> 00:00:46,433
They signal your brain, and your brain tells your tear glands to flush it out.

15
00:00:46,337 --> 00:00:48,220
So those tears aren't sadness.

16
00:00:48,110 --> 00:00:50,220
They're your eye washing itself.

17
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:52,113
And that's where your knife comes in.

18
00:00:52,017 --> 00:00:55,393
A blunt blade squashes the onion before it splits.

19
00:00:55,297 --> 00:00:59,750
In one lab test, it sprayed up to forty times more droplets than a sharp one.

20
00:00:59,670 --> 00:01:03,540
Onions make you cry because cutting mixes what they keep apart.

21
00:01:03,430 --> 00:01:06,700
Keep your knife sharp: less spray, fewer tears.
